 2. Types of Filters in Wireshark

Wireshark mainly uses two types of filters:

### Capture Filters

Capture filters determine **which packets should be captured**.

They are applied **before or during packet capture**.

Example:

```bash
host 192.168.1.10
```

This captures traffic involving the specified host.

Another example:

```bash
port 80
```

This captures traffic associated with port 80.

### Display Filters

Display filters determine **which already captured packets should be displayed**.

They do not remove packets from the capture file.

Example:

```text
ip.addr == 192.168.1.10
```

This displays packets involving the specified IP address.

### Simple Difference

**Capture Filter → What should be captured?**

**Display Filter → What should be displayed?**

---

 3. Display & Optimizing Filters

Display filters are extremely useful when a capture contains thousands or millions of packets.

Instead of manually inspecting every packet, filters allow specific traffic to be isolated.

For example:

```text
ip.addr == 192.168.1.10
```

Only traffic involving that IP will be displayed.

Filters can also be combined.

Example:

```text
ip.addr == 192.168.1.10 && tcp
```

This displays TCP traffic involving the specified IP.

Using filters effectively makes packet analysis:

* Faster
* More precise
* Easier to understand
* Less overwhelming

---

 4. Display Filters on IP Addresses

IP-based filtering is one of the most commonly used techniques in Wireshark.

### Filter traffic involving an IP

```text
ip.addr == 192.168.1.10
```

### Filter source IP

```text
ip.src == 192.168.1.10
```

### Filter destination IP

```text
ip.dst == 192.168.1.10
```

### Filter traffic between two IPs

```text
ip.addr == 192.168.1.10 && ip.addr == 192.168.1.20
```

These filters are useful when investigating communication between specific systems.

---

 5. Display Filters on Ports

Ports help identify which services or applications are communicating.

### HTTP

```text
tcp.port == 80
```

### HTTPS

```text
tcp.port == 443
```

### DNS

```text
udp.port == 53
```

### SSH

```text
tcp.port == 22
```

### Source port

```text
tcp.srcport == 443
```

### Destination port

```text
tcp.dstport == 80
```

Port filtering can help identify specific services and investigate unusual connections.

---

 6. Filtering Ports in Capture Filters

Capture filters can also be used to limit traffic based on ports.

Examples:

```bash
port 80
```

Capture traffic associated with port 80.

```bash
tcp port 443
```

Capture TCP traffic associated with HTTPS.

```bash
udp port 53
```

Capture UDP traffic associated with DNS.

This is useful when I already know which service or protocol I want to investigate.

---

 7. Capture Filters on Hosts

Host-based capture filters allow traffic to be captured based on a particular machine.

Example:

```bash
host 192.168.1.10
```

Traffic involving the host can be captured.

### Source host

```bash
src host 192.168.1.10
```

### Destination host

```bash
dst host 192.168.1.10
```

Host filters are useful during investigations where a particular system is suspected of generating or receiving suspicious traffic.

